1092 0 obj
<<
/A 2572 0 R
/Alt (The grouped bar chart displays normalised attention weight on the vertical axis from 0 to 0.40, in increments of 0.05. The horizontal axis shows DR Grade with five categories: No DR, Mild NPDR, Moderate NPDR, Severe NPDR, and PDR. Four sets of grouped bars represent quadrants Q1, Q2, Q3, and Q4, with error bars indicating standard deviation across test samples. For No DR and Mild NPDR, attention is concentrated primarily in Q1 and Q2, corresponding to the macular and perimacular regions, while Q3 and Q4 show lower values, reflecting that early DR lesions typically appear first in the posterior pole. For Moderate NPDR, the attention begins to spread more evenly across all four quadrants. For Severe NPDR, the attention distribution becomes notably uniform, with all four quadrants contributing similar weights between 0.22 and 0.28, consistent with the clinical definition that severe NPDR requires extensive lesions across all four retinal quadrants. For PDR, attention re-concentrates toward Q1 and Q3, corresponding to the superior and inferior regions near the optic disc where neovascularization most commonly develops. The overall pattern shifts from focal attention in early grades to diffuse distribution in severe grades, consistent with the clinical progression of diabetic retinopathy.)
/K 2
/P 43 0 R
/Pg 30 0 R
/S /Figure
/Type /StructElem
>>
endobj
